{"subjectId":"java","id":"arrays","title":"Arrays","group":"Core Language: Strings, Arrays & Methods","order":14,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A fixed-size, zero-indexed, contiguous container of one element type. Arrays are objects: they live on the heap and know their <code>length</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Array</strong> — an object holding <code>length</code> elements of the same type, accessed by index <code>0 … length-1</code> in O(1). Its size is fixed at creation; bounds are checked at runtime.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plus\"></i>Declaring and creating</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] a = new int[4];                  // all zeros\n        int[] b = {5, 3, 9};                   // array initializer\n        String[] names = new String[2];        // all null\n        boolean[] flags = new boolean[2];      // all false\n        double[] d = new double[]{1.5, 2.5};\n\n        a[0] = 42;\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a) + \" len=\" + a.length);\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(b) + \" \" + Arrays.toString(names) + \" \" + Arrays.toString(flags) + \" \" + Arrays.toString(d));\n        System.out.println(b);                 // prints type@hash — not the contents!\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The last line prints something like <code>[I@1b6d3586</code> — <code>[I</code> means “array of int”. Always print arrays with <code>Arrays.toString</code> (or <code>Arrays.deepToString</code> for nested arrays).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element type</th><th>Default value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>byte</code>, <code>short</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>long</code></td><td><code>0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>float</code>, <code>double</code></td><td><code>0.0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>char</code></td><td><code>'\\u0000'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td>Any reference type</td><td><code>null</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>2D and jagged arrays</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] grid = new int[2][3];          // 2 rows × 3 columns\n        grid[1][2] = 7;\n        System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(grid));\n\n        int[][] jagged = new int[3][];         // rows created separately\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; jagged.length; i++) {\n            jagged[i] = new int[i + 1];\n            Arrays.fill(jagged[i], i);\n        }\n        System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(jagged));\n\n        int[][] m = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};\n        int total = 0;\n        for (int[] row : m) for (int x : row) total += x;\n        System.out.println(\"total=\" + total + \" rows=\" + m.length + \" cols=\" + m[0].length);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 7]]\n[[0], [1, 1], [2, 2, 2]]\ntotal=10 rows=2 cols=2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tool\"></i>The <code>java.util.Arrays</code> toolbox</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] a = {5, 2, 8, 1, 9};\n        Arrays.sort(a);                                        // dual-pivot quicksort for primitives\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));\n        System.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(a, 8));         // array must be sorted\n        int[] bigger = Arrays.copyOf(a, 7);                    // pads with zeros\n        int[] mid = Arrays.copyOfRange(a, 1, 3);\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(bigger) + \" \" + Arrays.toString(mid));\n\n        int[] x = {1, 2}, y = {1, 2};\n        System.out.println((x == y) + \" \" + x.equals(y) + \" \" + Arrays.equals(x, y));\n\n        Integer[] boxed = {3, 1, 2};\n        Arrays.sort(boxed, (p, q) -&gt; q - p);                   // comparator needs objects\n        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(boxed));\n        System.out.println(Arrays.stream(a).sum() + \" \" + Arrays.stream(a).max().getAsInt());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[1, 2, 5, 8, 9]\n3\n[1, 2, 5, 8, 9, 0, 0] [2, 5]\nfalse false true\n[3, 2, 1]\n25 9</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>array.equals(other)</code> is inherited from <code>Object</code> — it compares references. Use <code>Arrays.equals</code> (or <code>Arrays.deepEquals</code> for 2D).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i><code>Arrays.asList</code> pitfalls</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String[] arr = {\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"};\n        List&lt;String&gt; view = Arrays.asList(arr);     // fixed-size view backed by arr\n        view.set(0, \"z\");\n        System.out.println(arr[0]);                  // the array changed too\n        try {\n            view.add(\"d\");\n        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"cannot add: \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName());\n        }\n\n        int[] prim = {1, 2, 3};\n        System.out.println(Arrays.asList(prim).size());   // List&lt;int[]&gt; with ONE element\n        List&lt;String&gt; copy = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(Arrays.asList(arr));   // real, growable list\n        copy.add(\"d\");\n        System.out.println(copy);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>z\ncannot add: UnsupportedOperationException\n1\n[z, b, c, d]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>Arrays are covariant (a design flaw)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Object[] objs = new String[2];      // compiles: String[] is an Object[]\n        objs[0] = \"fine\";\n        try {\n            objs[1] = 42;                   // runtime check fails\n        } catch (ArrayStoreException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"ArrayStoreException: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ArrayStoreException: java.lang.Integer</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between length, length() and size()?</summary><div><p><code>array.length</code> is a field of arrays; <code>string.length()</code> is a method on <code>String</code>; <code>collection.size()</code> is a method on collections.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can an array's size change?</summary><div><p>No. To grow, allocate a bigger array and copy (<code>Arrays.copyOf</code>, <code>System.arraycopy</code>) — which is exactly what <code>ArrayList</code> does internally.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why can't you create a generic array like new T[n]?</summary><div><p>Arrays check element types at runtime (they are <em>reified</em>), but generic types are erased. The JVM wouldn't know what <code>T</code> is, so <code>new T[]</code> and <code>new List&lt;String&gt;[]</code> are compile errors. Use <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>(T[]) new Object[n]</code> with care.</p></div></details></section>"}